add('care-orientation', 'Start here · Care, connection and professional boundaries', '''
<p>Good care begins with a respectful relationship, a clear role and the willingness to ask for help. This guide introduces the approaches our agencies use and the judgment that keeps care within each person’s competence.</p>
<p><strong>This is familiarization, not authorization to practice.</strong> Reading, asking the guide questions, completing its knowledge check or receiving a completion record does not certify a technique, establish clinical competence, confer a professional title or authorize independent treatment. Examples show how to think and communicate; they are not a treatment protocol for a particular client.</p>
<p>Before providing any activity, confirm your assigned role, education, relevant training, demonstrated skills, supervision, client plan and applicable legal and payer requirements. A supervisor’s approval cannot expand a lawful scope of practice. Licensed clinicians also remain responsible for their own competence.</p>
<p>The guide is available in the app’s Library, onboarding, My Learning/Plotline and on-demand learning. Required onboarding and supervisor- or administrator-assigned learning use the app’s paid-time workflow. Optional review and retakes are on your own time. Completion records document orientation only.</p>
<p>Read this with the current company Workplace Handbook and Provider’s Guide. For an internal policy conflict, the current published Workplace Handbook controls, subject to law, professional scope and payer requirements. Bring a conflict to the clinical lead; do not interpret it as permission to expand services.</p>
<p><a href="/workplace-handbook">Workplace Handbook</a> · <a href="/library?companyDocument=provider-guide#provider-guide-overview">Provider’s Guide</a></p>''')
add('company-care-context', 'Our company’s care setting', '<p>Company-specific service scope is supplied with each edition.</p>')
add('care-role-map', 'Understand the role you hold', '''
<ul><li><strong>Facilitators:</strong> our bachelor’s-level staff in the approved clinical support role. They introduce themselves as facilitators, not counselors or therapists. They support specifically authorized activities within their education, training and demonstrated competence, under qualified licensed oversight.</li>
<li><strong>Students and pre-licensed clinicians:</strong> use the accurate student, intern or candidate title and the scope approved for their actual program, credential and assignment. A student agreement or a graduate degree does not confer independent licensure.</li>
<li><strong>Licensed clinicians:</strong> assess, diagnose, plan and provide care only within their particular license, training and competence. They retain accountability for clinical decisions they make or oversee.</li>
<li><strong>Administrative and billing staff:</strong> verify service and claim requirements; they do not authorize a clinical activity outside someone’s scope.</li></ul>
<p>A code’s service description and a staff member’s job title answer different questions. H0004 includes the words counseling and therapy; that wording does not change a facilitator’s title, competence or scope.</p>''')
add('facilitator-pathway', 'Bachelor’s-level facilitation · Colorado H0004', '''
<p>Our facilitator pathway aims to improve access to timely, low-acuity support, early recognition of concerns and appropriate referral. Facilitators can help people engage with care and rehearse approved skills while licensed clinicians remain responsible for clinical oversight.</p>
<p>These are agency practice boundaries. H0004 is a Medicaid service code, not a license or an unrestricted preventive-care benefit. An access mission does not waive assessment, medical necessity, plan, consent, coverage or provider qualifications.</p>''')
add('facilitator-scope', 'Facilitator scope, titles and express approval', '''
<p><strong>Use the title facilitator. Do not introduce or advertise yourself as a counselor or therapist in this bachelor’s-level role.</strong> Accurately identify your education and licensed oversight without implying that the supervisor’s license is your own.</p>
<p>Work only within your actual education, relevant skills, training and demonstrated competence. Assignment to the role does not authorize every method discussed in this guide. Our default facilitator work is low-acuity, plan-approved support; independent psychotherapy, diagnosis, specialist treatment and crisis management are outside this assignment.</p>
<p><strong>Do not independently establish, select, change or treat a primary diagnosis.</strong> Do not begin diagnosis-targeted treatment activities without the express, documented approval of the appropriately qualified, fully licensed clinician responsible for the case. That clinician must determine clinical appropriateness and the plan, verify the facilitator’s competence and specify the authorized activities and oversight. Approval never permits an activity outside legal scope, payer eligibility or demonstrated competence.</p>
<p>A client’s existing diagnosis is not an automatic exclusion from all facilitated support, nor is it permission to treat the condition. Obtain the required case-specific approval first. Do not omit, invent or change a diagnosis to make a claim pass. Refer assessment and diagnostic decisions to the qualified clinician.</p>
<p>When a need exceeds your approved role, stop the unsupported activity, contact the clinical lead and arrange the appropriate handoff. Escalate urgent safety concerns immediately rather than waiting for routine approval.</p>''', 'facilitator-pathway')
add('licensed-oversight', 'Licensed oversight and the two-year agency minimum', f'''
<p><strong>Agency requirement:</strong> all facilitator clinical work must have oversight by a fully licensed provider with at least two years in the field, relevant competence and authority to oversee the actual service. This is a minimum agency requirement, not a statement that two years alone satisfies Colorado law or Medicaid requirements.</p>
<p>Before assignment, the clinical lead verifies the supervisor’s current license, applicable board requirements, relevant experience and payer qualifications. Follow any stricter requirements. Keep the designated supervisor, backup contact, approval boundaries and consultation arrangements in the app’s authorized supervision and staff records. If appropriate oversight is unavailable, do not start an unsupported service.</p>
<p><strong>Medicaid rendering oversight:</strong> HCPF’s published RPO policy also requires an eligible active Colorado license, no licensing restrictions/conditions/stipulations in the preceding two years, Health First Colorado enrollment, a current CAQH profile and applicable RAE credentialing. It sets minimum oversight of one hour per 20 Medicaid billable hours for unlicensed professionals and one per 40 for pre-licensed clinicians. Documentation review, applicable co-signatures and access to clinical support for emergencies are part of oversight. These billing-oversight minimums do not replace agency supervision or licensing-board rules.</p>
<p>The agency’s two-year experience floor and HCPF’s two-year restriction history are different requirements. Administration must verify which oversight category applies; do not classify a bachelor’s-level facilitator as a pre-licensed clinician merely because they plan to attend graduate school.</p>
<p>References: {link(RPO, 'HCPF Rendering Provider Oversight Policy')} and {link(FAQ, 'January 2026 RPO FAQs')}. Check the applicable policy and payer agreement for the service date.</p>''', 'facilitator-pathway')
add('h0004-service', 'H0004 · Service definition and billing boundaries', f'''
<p>The January 2026 Colorado SBHS manual, page 53, lists bachelor’s-level staff among H0004 service providers. It describes individual counseling/therapy for assessed problems included in a treatment/service plan, in 15-minute units, with an 8-minute minimum in the unit table. Necessary, clinically justified plan-based telephone contact may qualify. Crisis intervention is identified separately as H2011.</p>
<p><strong>Agency application:</strong> billing staff must verify the current manual, member coverage, payer/RAE contract, service-provider definition, rendering/billing provider eligibility, authorizations, setting, modality, documentation and time rules before submitting a claim. Listing bachelor’s-level staff does not mean every degree, agency or visit qualifies.</p>
<p>Record actual service time in the app. A scheduled half-hour is not proof that a payable half-hour occurred. This guide does not configure units, authorize a claim or select a code for a particular client. Do not bill tutoring, ordinary recreation, supervision, note writing or a missed appointment as delivered H0004 counseling. Do not substitute H2011 merely because a concern arose; qualified staff and billing must determine the actual service.</p>
<p>Reference: {link(MANUAL, 'HCPF SBHS manual · January 2026, H0004, page 53')}. This dated reference is not a guarantee of coverage or that no subsequent update exists. Use {link('https://hcpf.colorado.gov/sbhs-billing-manual', 'HCPF’s current manual index')} and the payer’s requirements for the service date.</p>''', 'facilitator-pathway')
add('facilitator-readiness', 'Before a facilitator sees a client', '''
<ol><li>Confirm the role, education and relevant training have been reviewed and the proposed skills have been observed and approved by the licensed clinical lead.</li>
<li>Confirm the case is appropriate for facilitated low-acuity support, the necessary assessment is complete, and the responsible clinician has approved the plan and the specific activities. Obtain express documented approval for diagnosis-targeted work.</li>
<li>Verify consent and who may receive information. Administration and billing confirm eligibility and required authorizations.</li>
<li>Know the supervisor and backup, when to consult, what requires co-signature and how to escalate a safety concern. Agree on what should cause the activity to stop or the case to be reassessed.</li>
<li>Use the app for the approved schedule, care plan, notes, supervision records and actual time. A guide-completion badge is not the clinical approval record.</li></ol>
<p>An approval record should identify the approving clinician, their credentials, date, client/plan, specific activities, evidence of competence, restrictions and review arrangements. Use authorized records with appropriate access; do not put identifiable case details into the guide’s question box.</p>''', 'facilitator-pathway')
add('facilitator-examples', 'Facilitation in practice · Support, consult, hand off', '''
<h3>An approved support activity</h3><p>A client’s licensed clinician has approved practicing how to ask for a break when overwhelmed. A facilitator who has demonstrated this skill offers a short rehearsal, respects the client’s choice to stop, describes the actual response in the app and brings concerns back to supervision. This is an example of following an approved plan, not independent diagnosis or treatment selection.</p>
<h3>A request that needs approval</h3><p>A family asks the facilitator to treat a child’s primary anxiety diagnosis using CBT. The facilitator explains their role and consults the responsible licensed clinician before any diagnosis-targeted work. A book chapter, parent request or general approval to “use CBT” does not establish competence for a protocol.</p>
<h3>A change that needs immediate escalation</h3><p>A client discloses current self-harm danger, abuse or another urgent concern. The facilitator follows the emergency/reporting procedures and contacts qualified clinical support promptly. Do not continue the planned low-acuity exercise or wait for the next scheduled supervision meeting.</p>''', 'facilitator-pathway')
add('rbt-comparison', 'Why a short course is not a credential', f'''
<p>The RBT pathway illustrates the importance of a defined role, assessed competence and ongoing oversight. It is a separate behavior-analysis credential; it does not establish a counseling or H0004 qualification.</p>
<p>BACB’s June 2026 handbook lists high-school-level education or equivalent, age and background requirements, a specified 40-hour course, an initial competency assessment and an examination. Ongoing supervision and credential-maintenance requirements also apply. The 40-hour course alone does not make someone an RBT.</p>
<p>Likewise, a bachelor’s degree, this guide and a knowledge check do not authorize clinical practice. Our facilitator pathway requires role-specific education and training review, observed competence, documented approval, eligible services and qualified licensed oversight. We do not equate this guide to RBT training or award an RBT credential.</p>
<p>Reference: {link(BACB, 'BACB RBT Handbook · requirements and supervision')}.</p>''', 'facilitator-pathway')

add('connection', 'Build a relationship that respects the person', '<p>Start with curiosity, dignity and understandable language. These foundational skills support many roles, but the purpose, content and limits of each encounter still follow the approved care plan.</p>')
add('development-and-access', 'Development, culture and access', '''
<p>Use developmental information as a starting point, not a rigid age rule. Ask about the person’s communication, sensory, language, cultural and accessibility preferences. Avoid assuming that eye contact, speech, play style, disability, family structure or cultural difference tells you what someone feels.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Younger children:</strong> short, concrete explanations, predictable transitions and choice of age-appropriate activities can support participation. Check understanding and assent alongside lawful consent.</li><li><strong>School-age children:</strong> invite their own words, interests and goals; explain each activity and offer manageable choices. An academic concern can prompt a care conversation, but ordinary tutoring is not automatically a clinical service.</li><li><strong>Adolescents:</strong> respect autonomy, identity, peer relationships and privacy. Explain the actual limits of confidentiality without assuming every young person or family has the same needs.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> ask about their priorities and preferences directly. Do not carry child-focused activities or assumptions into adult care without a reason and consent.</li></ul>
<p>Arrange approved language/access supports through the app. Ask supervision when an adaptation changes the intervention or is beyond your training. A person’s refusal is information to understand, not an invitation to force participation.</p>''', 'connection')
add('rapport-and-listening', 'Rapport, active listening and supportive reflection', f'''
<p>Introduce your actual role, explain the purpose of the meeting and ask what would help the person participate. Listen without planning a correction. Allow silence and let the person finish. Warmth and unconditional positive regard mean respect for the person; they do not require agreement with unsafe actions or promises of absolute secrecy.</p>
<p>Reflect what you heard tentatively and invite correction: <em>“It sounds like being left out was upsetting. Did I understand?”</em> If the person corrects you, accept the correction. Do not claim that you know their feelings from their posture or from your own emotional response.</p>
<p><strong>Illustrative exchange:</strong> “Nobody wants me on their team.” A helpful response might be, “You wanted to be included, and that felt disappointing. What happened?” Avoid guaranteeing “Everyone likes you,” diagnosing rejection sensitivity or telling them to replace their feeling with a positive thought.</p>
<p>These agency examples illustrate respectful communication. {link(SAMHSA, 'SAMHSA’s communication guidance')} describes nonjudgmental listening, open questions, supportive responses and summaries. It is a basic helping resource, not a credential or a client-specific treatment plan.</p>''', 'connection')
add('questions-and-summaries', 'Open questions, affirmations and summaries', f'''
<p>Use one understandable question at a time. Ask <em>“What would you like me to understand?”</em> or <em>“What helped even a little?”</em> without pressuring for a particular answer. Closed questions sometimes clarify a fact; repeated interrogation can get in the way of listening. Do not lead an abuse disclosure or investigate it yourself.</p>
<p>An affirmation names a real effort or strength: <em>“You asked for support even though it felt difficult.”</em> It is not praise designed to secure compliance. A summary checks shared understanding: <em>“You want help speaking up, and you would like to start with a short practice. What have I missed?”</em></p>
<p>Keep the person’s language and allow disagreement. Do not edit a summary to make the person seem more motivated or improved than they report. Asking for their preferred next step supports collaboration without transferring clinical decisions outside your role.</p>
<p>{link(TIP, 'SAMHSA TIP 35, chapter 3')} discusses open questions, affirmations, reflective listening and summaries in a substance-use-treatment context. These communication concepts inform this orientation; this guide is not a complete motivational interviewing course and does not authorize substance-use treatment.</p>''', 'connection')
add('observation-and-regulation', 'Notice, ask and offer support', '''
<p>Moment awareness means noticing the interaction while managing your own response. Describe observable behavior without assigning meaning: <em>“I noticed the game stopped. Would you like to tell me about that, take a pause, or do something else?”</em> A facilitator’s frustration or tension is a cue to self-reflect and consult, not evidence of what a client feels.</p>
<p>Co-regulation in this orientation means a steady, respectful presence and choices within the approved plan. You might offer a quiet pause or an already approved calming activity. Do not require eye contact, touch, closed eyes, breathing exercises or personal disclosure. If an activity increases distress, stop and consult.</p>
<p>Deep breathing, mindfulness, grounding, guided imagery, visualization, progressive muscle relaxation and other relaxation methods are not interchangeable or suitable for everyone. Their inclusion here is awareness only. Use a particular exercise only when its appropriateness and your competence have been established. Do not improvise trauma processing, restraint or crisis interventions.</p>''', 'connection')
add('strengths-and-goals', 'Strengths, hope and collaborative goals', '''
<p>Ask what matters to the person, what has helped before and what support they want. Strengths can include persistence, care for others, creativity or willingness to ask for help. Avoid forced gratitude, affirmations the person does not believe, or reassurance that dismisses real discrimination, loss or danger.</p>
<p>A small goal might be to practice asking for help during an overwhelming transition. The licensed clinician determines how that goal fits the treatment plan; a facilitator can support an approved rehearsal and report what happened. Do not choose a clinical goal simply because it is easy to count.</p>
<p>Solution-focused approaches explore a preferred future and manageable steps. Strengths-based approaches attend to resources as well as problems. Familiarity with these ideas does not authorize independent solution-focused therapy or treatment planning.</p>''', 'connection')

add('approaches', 'A window into approaches to care', '<p>The following descriptions explain vocabulary you may hear in supervision and team discussion. Each method has its own indications, training and practice requirements. A familiar activity is not necessarily delivery of a named therapy. Do not use this guide as a session-by-session treatment manual.</p>')
add('play-and-spt', 'Play-based communication and Synergetic Play Therapy', '''
<p>Children may communicate through play as well as words. An approved play-based communication activity can help a person participate, but using toys does not make an encounter play therapy. Do not infer abuse, a diagnosis or family dynamics from a toy choice or a scene.</p>
<p><strong>Illustrative response:</strong> a child builds a wall around a toy. Rather than announcing “You feel trapped at home,” ask, “Would you like to tell me about what you made?” Accept that they may not want to explain.</p>
<p>Synergetic Play Therapy (SPT) is a specific play-therapy approach discussed in the original manual. Its attention to the therapeutic relationship, regulation and the practitioner’s awareness belongs within method-specific education, supervision and competence. This guide does not reproduce an SPT curriculum or confer certification.</p>
<p>Facilitators must not represent basic play activities, attentive observation or a calm presence as SPT or specialist play therapy. Refer questions about delivering or describing these therapies to a clinician with the appropriate training. Personal feelings during a session are not diagnostic evidence about a client.</p>''', 'approaches')
add('creative-expression', 'Creative expression, art and music', '''
<p>A person may choose drawing, storytelling or an approved music activity to help express something. Ask what their work means to them; do not interpret colors, images, lyrics or silence as a diagnostic finding.</p>
<p><strong>Illustrative invitation:</strong> “Would you rather describe that with words, draw something, or leave it for another time?” Follow the approved plan, their choice and the privacy rules for anything created.</p>
<p>Ordinary creative expression is distinct from professional art therapy or music therapy. Staff must have the appropriate qualifications, training and agency authorization before representing or delivering those specialized services. A facilitator should accurately describe the actual activity rather than use a specialist title to make a note sound clinical.</p>
<p>Recreation, art classes, music listening and tutoring are not automatically reimbursable behavioral health care. Billing follows the actual authorized service, not the materials used.</p>''', 'approaches')
add('behavioral-support', 'Behavioral supports, reinforcement and tracking', '''
<p>Some plans include noticing helpful actions, tracking a specific goal or rehearsing an alternative response. The responsible clinician selects and reviews the intervention. A facilitator may carry out a specifically approved support only after demonstrating competence.</p>
<p>A progress chart should record what actually occurred, not label a person good or bad. Discuss how the person experiences a reward or tracking system. Preserve dignity, privacy and meaningful choice. Do not withhold basic needs, use humiliation, improvise punishment or apply an unapproved behavior-reduction procedure.</p>
<p>Token systems, behavior plans, behavioral activation and behavior analysis require more than a definition in a guide. An activity suggested for motivation can be inappropriate in another context. Bring refusal, distress, worsening behavior or unclear results to supervision instead of escalating consequences.</p>
<p>Example: the client practices the plan-approved way to request a break. Record whether they attempted it, their reported experience and any support needed. Do not claim a disorder improved because one task was completed.</p>''', 'approaches')
add('cognitive-approaches', 'CBT, reframing and restructuring · Orientation only', '''
<p>Cognitive behavioral approaches explore relationships among thoughts, feelings, physical experiences and actions. Reframing and cognitive restructuring are terms for considering interpretations; they do not mean replacing every difficult thought with a positive one. Real danger, loss, injustice and distress require understanding, not automatic contradiction.</p>
<p>The original manual’s repeated thought worksheets are consolidated here as an illustration for discussion with a supervisor. They are not a protocol, competency assessment or instruction to independently deliver CBT. A facilitator may rehearse a specific clinician-approved skill only within demonstrated competence and the client’s plan.</p>
<p><strong>Illustrative conversation:</strong> a client says, “If I ask for help, everyone will think I’m stupid.” Begin by listening. If authorized and appropriate, an invitation might be, “Would it be useful to think together about what might happen if you asked someone you trust?” The client may decline or describe a real past experience that changes the plan.</p>
<p>If the approved plan uses self-ratings, record the client’s actual ratings. Do not supply a lower number after an exercise or imply that a particular change should occur. Discuss discomfort, worsening symptoms or a mismatch with the plan with the responsible clinician.</p>
<p>Clinical formulation, exposure work, trauma-focused protocols and independent treatment selection are not authorized by this orientation. A knowledge-check pass does not change that boundary.</p>''', 'approaches')
add('everyday-skill-practice', 'Communication, boundaries and everyday skill rehearsal', '''
<p>Approved support may include practicing how to ask a question, express a preference, request a break or identify a trusted support. Communication skills, assertiveness, boundary development, conflict resolution, social skills, structured problem solving and time management are broad categories, not automatic authorizations or billing codes.</p>
<p>Use a simple fictional role-play or a client-chosen example that fits the plan. Ask permission before practicing and invite feedback afterward. Do not rehearse traumatic events, pressure disclosure or tell a person to confront someone unsafe. Family and relationship-pattern work can require a different clinician or service.</p>
<p>Homework or scheduling examples can help explain an approved coping goal, but actual instruction in reading, math or schoolwork remains tutoring. Do not rename tutoring “skill development” to bill insurance. Community support and physical-wellness planning should connect people to suitable resources, not become medical, nutrition or exercise advice outside your role.</p>''', 'approaches')
add('trauma-informed-approach', 'Trauma-informed presence and specialist trauma care', '''
<p>Our agency approach is to make participation predictable, explain choices, respect boundaries and avoid unnecessary pressure. Ask what feels useful or uncomfortable without demanding a trauma history. Someone may experience a calming activity as distressing; their response matters more than the activity’s name.</p>
<p>Trauma-informed care is not the same as delivering trauma treatment. Facilitators must not conduct trauma narratives, exposure, memory-recovery work or trauma-processing protocols on the basis of this guide. A disclosure does not authorize investigation, leading questions or an improvised intervention.</p>
<p>Listen without judgment, acknowledge the person’s experience and follow the safety, reporting and consultation pathway. Do not promise secrecy or require a detailed account before seeking help. The qualified clinician determines assessment, level of care and specialist treatment needs.</p>''', 'approaches')

add('recognition-and-response', 'Recognize concerns and connect to the right care', '<p>Observation supports a clinical team; it does not establish a diagnosis. A change can have many explanations, including medical, developmental, environmental or safety needs. Facilitators describe what they observe, listen to what the client reports and consult the licensed clinician.</p>')
add('anxiety-and-stress', 'Anxiety and stress · Notice without diagnosing', '''
<p>The original manual describes worry, avoidance, physical complaints, restlessness and difficulty concentrating. These are possible concerns to discuss, not a checklist that proves anxiety. New or severe physical symptoms may need medical assessment.</p>
<p>A younger child might have trouble with a transition; a teen might describe pressure about peers or the future; an adult may describe demands at work or home. Ask about the person’s experience rather than assigning an age-based explanation.</p>
<p>Follow the existing plan and use only approved support. Bring persistent distress, significant impairment, a changed presentation or an ineffective activity to the clinician for reassessment. Do not independently begin exposure, cognitive restructuring or treatment for a presumed anxiety diagnosis.</p>''', 'recognition-and-response')
add('mood-and-self-worth', 'Low mood, self-worth and withdrawal', '''
<p>Changes in participation, sleep, energy, interest or self-talk can be reasons for a clinical conversation. They do not establish depression. Avoid assuming irritability is defiance or that withdrawal can be resolved by praise, a gratitude list or a preferred activity.</p>
<p>Offer respectful listening and the specific support in the approved plan. Strengths work should acknowledge real effort without insisting the person feels better. Behavioral activation or other diagnosis-focused treatment requires the responsible clinician’s assessment, authorization and a competent provider.</p>
<p>Statements about self-harm, suicide, hopelessness with safety concerns or harm to others require prompt clinical escalation and the applicable emergency response. A facilitator must not independently declare someone low risk or continue routine exercises as a substitute for assessment.</p>''', 'recognition-and-response')
add('relationships-and-safety', 'Families, peers, relationships and safety', '''
<p>Listen to concerns about conflict, exclusion, bullying, identity or family stress without taking sides or assuming the same family structure and values for everyone. Ask what support is wanted, within the approved role.</p>
<p>Practice a communication skill only if it is safe and authorized. Mediation is not automatically appropriate where there is abuse, coercion or a power imbalance. A facilitator does not independently provide family therapy, interpret custody orders or promise what another person will do.</p>
<p>Concerns about running away, violence, exploitation, abuse or unsafe living conditions need timely consultation and applicable reporting or emergency action. Document observable facts and the client’s words in the app; do not investigate or contact a potentially unsafe person without an approved lawful plan.</p>''', 'recognition-and-response')
add('urgent-concerns', 'When to stop and get immediate help', '''
<p>If there is immediate danger or a medical emergency, activate the applicable emergency response, including 911 when needed, and contact qualified clinical support. Do not wait for routine supervision, a quiz answer or a handbook chatbot. Follow the current company emergency procedures for the actual setting.</p>
<p>Escalate suicidal or self-harm concerns, threats, suspected abuse, severe disorientation, acute intoxication or a marked change beyond your competence. Stay within your training and the emergency plan; do not independently assess risk, diagnose, restrain or improvise specialist crisis treatment.</p>
<p>Arrange a clear handoff to the responsible clinician or emergency responder and document facts, actions, timing and contacts in the app. Internal notification does not replace a legally required external report. Routine approval requirements must never delay emergency action or required reporting.</p>''', 'recognition-and-response')

add('planning-and-records', 'Care plans, documentation and truthful communication', '<p>A clear record connects the person’s goals, the approved activity, what happened and what needs follow-up. Use the app for care records, scheduling, supervision and timekeeping.</p>')
add('goals-and-plan', 'Follow the plan and report progress', '''
<p>The qualified clinician is responsible for clinical assessment, diagnostic decisions and treatment planning within their role. Invite the client’s priorities and authorized family participation. Goals should be understandable, meaningful and reviewable, with a planned approach and follow-up.</p>
<p>Facilitators follow the approved plan and contribute observations. They do not independently add a diagnosis, select a new therapy, change intensity, discharge a client or rewrite the plan when an exercise does not work. Ask the responsible clinician to review a mismatch, changed need or lack of progress.</p>
<p>Example of an approved rehearsal goal: the person wants to practice asking for a pause during a stressful conversation. Note what was practiced, their response and the support used. The clinician determines whether the activity and goal remain appropriate.</p>
<p>Outcome measures can inform a qualified review when authorized and suitable. A score alone is not a diagnosis, risk determination or permission to expand a role. Use only the measures actually available and approved in the app.</p>''', 'planning-and-records')
add('care-documentation', 'Document what actually happened in the app', '''
<p>Record the actual date, service location/modality, time/duration, relevant plan goal, approved activity, client response, concerns and next steps, with your accurate identity, role and required signatures. Record consultation and required co-signature through the authorized workflow.</p>
<p><strong>Illustrative note fragment, not a complete billable note:</strong> “Practiced the plan-approved request for a break using a fictional conversation. Client chose to try once and said it felt awkward. Paused on request. Sent the observation to the supervising clinician for review.” Add required encounter details and only facts that occurred.</p>
<p>Do not copy sample improvement, claim to deliver CBT/SPT/art therapy when you did not, reuse someone else’s credentials, or label ordinary tutoring as clinical care. Separate client statements from observations and clinical conclusions. A facilitator reports concerns without independently making diagnostic conclusions.</p>
<p>Use the app’s authorized records, forms and timekeeping. Do not create parallel Google Forms, spreadsheets, personal documents or external time logs. Correct errors through the record’s approved amendment workflow; do not erase work or rewrite history to match a billing expectation. Record all actual work time under the current compensation policy even when a clinical claim cannot be submitted.</p>
<p>Do not enter client names, identifying details or case records into the guide’s general question box. It explains document content and cannot review a client chart or approve care.</p>''', 'planning-and-records')
add('explain-your-role', 'Explain care honestly to clients and families', '''
<p>Use plain language, your accurate title and realistic expectations. A facilitator might say: <em>“I’m a facilitator. I support specific activities in the plan under our licensed clinician’s oversight. I can help you connect with that clinician about assessment or treatment questions.”</em></p>
<p>Describe what you are authorized to do and how supervision works. Do not imply you are a therapist, specialist or independently licensed because a billing description uses those words. Do not promise a result, guaranteed coverage or a particular number of visits.</p>
<p>Use clearly fictional examples when explaining care. Do not market a method through identifiable client stories, invented success rates or another person’s credentials. A qualified clinician explains specialist treatment and obtains the appropriate informed consent. The client’s actual consent and disclosure pathway controls family updates.</p>''', 'planning-and-records')

add('ethical-practice', 'Privacy, safety and professional judgment', '<p>Respectful relationships depend on accurate explanations and clear boundaries. Use the current handbook and qualified consultation for case-specific legal or clinical decisions.</p>')
add('consent-and-privacy', 'Consent and confidentiality, including minors', '''
<p>Before routine care or outreach, verify who can consent, the authorized communication pathway, applicable releases and any custody or minor-consent issues. A referral, school contact or insurance card does not establish authority by itself.</p>
<p>The original manual’s blanket “12 and older means confidential” wording must not be used. Colorado minor-consent and disclosure rules depend on the service, provider qualifications, circumstances and applicable law. A facilitator must not independently decide minor-consent eligibility or promise that every parent can—or cannot—receive information. Obtain review by the qualified clinician and administration.</p>
<p>An age-appropriate explanation might be: <em>“We take your privacy seriously. There are limits, including situations where we must get help or share information as the law requires. Let’s talk with the clinician about what applies to your care.”</em> Do not promise that the client can always veto a necessary disclosure or that every concern automatically goes to a parent.</p>
<p>Discuss permitted family participation collaboratively. Share only through authorized channels and record the decision. Consult the <a href="/workplace-handbook">current company Workplace Handbook</a> and Provider’s Guide for the approved workflow.</p>''', 'ethical-practice')
add('required-reporting', 'Required reporting and internal escalation', f'''
<p>Know the reporting duties for your role and jurisdiction before seeing clients. In Colorado, current CDHS guidance states that mandatory reporters must report suspected child abuse or neglect within 24 hours to the hotline or local law enforcement. Call 911 for an emergency. Telling a supervisor does not discharge an individual reporting duty.</p>
<p><strong>Agency practice:</strong> act promptly and follow any stricter applicable requirement. Do not wait for permission, complete an investigation or use an internal app message as a substitute for the legally required report. Consult qualified support alongside the response without delaying it.</p>
<p>Colorado’s Child Abuse and Neglect Hotline is 844-264-5437. Document the report and internal follow-up in the appropriate restricted app record. Explain limits of confidentiality accurately and involve the client when safe and permitted; do not promise to keep an abuse disclosure secret.</p>
<p>Reference: {link(REPORT, 'Colorado Department of Human Services · CO4Kids mandatory reporting guidance')}. Reporting to child protection and responding to a clinical safety emergency are related but distinct duties.</p>''', 'ethical-practice')
add('boundaries-and-culture', 'Boundaries, cultural humility and collaboration', '''
<p>Maintain professional contact channels, appropriate self-disclosure and the boundaries in the handbook. Bring potential dual relationships, gifts, social-media contact, conflicts of interest or overlapping family/community roles to the clinical lead. Do not decide that a relationship is safe simply because the community is small.</p>
<p>Ask about language, identity, beliefs, family and access needs without expecting a person to represent an entire culture. Invite correction and adapt within your competence. An interpreter or accessibility support is not a substitute decision-maker.</p>
<p>Seek consultation when personal reactions or assumptions influence care. Networking, observation and discussion require permission and confidentiality protections. Use approved supervision spaces and minimum necessary information; the Library’s general Q&amp;A is not a clinical case conference.</p>''', 'ethical-practice')

add('learning-and-resources', 'Keep learning, with supervision', '<p>Orientation is a starting point. Responsible development includes instruction, observed practice, feedback, explicit authorization and continued review.</p>')
add('competence-and-next-steps', 'From familiarity to demonstrated competence', f'''
<ol><li><strong>Identify the role and need.</strong> The clinical lead determines what skills fit the actual assignment and clients.</li><li><strong>Complete suitable instruction.</strong> Use approved, role-appropriate learning with prerequisites and method-specific supervision where needed.</li><li><strong>Practice safely.</strong> Use fictional role-plays or authorized observation, with consent and confidentiality safeguards. Practicing a disclosure response is different from investigating a real disclosure.</li><li><strong>Demonstrate competence.</strong> A qualified supervisor observes performance, gives feedback and documents any limits or additional learning needed.</li><li><strong>Receive express authorization and oversight.</strong> Confirm the permitted activity, client plan, applicable rules and ongoing review. Stop and consult when circumstances exceed that approval.</li></ol>
<p>Passing this guide’s knowledge check records understanding of the guide. It is not a skills assessment, professional certification, continuing-education credit or clinical authorization.</p>
<p>{link(WHO, 'WHO/UNICEF foundational helping skills manual')} is a reference for trainers and supervisors on observed competencies such as communication, empathy and collaboration. The cited edition focuses on helping adults; it is not a child-treatment curriculum or Colorado scope authority. Reference access does not mean its assessments or materials have been licensed for reproduction in this commercial app.</p>''', 'learning-and-resources')
add('practice-reflections', 'Supervision discussion examples', '''
<ul><li><strong>First introduction:</strong> explain your actual role, oversight and privacy limits without specialist claims.</li><li><strong>A person declines an activity:</strong> acknowledge the choice and ask what would help; identify when a plan review is needed.</li><li><strong>A request to use a new technique:</strong> explain why reading about it is insufficient and name the approval and competency steps.</li><li><strong>A safety disclosure:</strong> identify immediate response and reporting routes without acting as an investigator or independent crisis clinician.</li><li><strong>A family asks for details:</strong> verify the lawful disclosure pathway rather than using age alone.</li></ul>
<p>Use fictional scenarios. The supervisor gives feedback on observable communication and judgment and decides the next learning step. These discussion prompts are not standardized assessment instruments or substitute clinical credentials.</p>
<p>Build an ongoing learning plan in the app’s training and supervision workflows. Include relevant skills, review dates, feedback and support for your own wellbeing. Ask for help early when workload or personal stress interferes with safe practice.</p>''', 'learning-and-resources')
add('skills-index', 'Appendix · Find a skill or approach', '<p>This index consolidates repeated intervention lists from the original manual. Each link includes the relevant role limits. An item’s presence does not authorize its use.</p>' + '<ul>' + ''.join(f'<li>{internal(slug, title)}</li>' for slug,title in [
('rapport-and-listening','Active listening, rapport, empathetic responses, supportive reflection and unconditional positive regard'),
('questions-and-summaries','Open-ended questions, affirmations, summarizing and interactive feedback'),
('observation-and-regulation','Moment awareness, observational statements, co-regulation, mindfulness, deep breathing, grounding, relaxation, imagery and progressive muscle relaxation'),
('strengths-and-goals','Solution-focused goals, strengths, hope, gratitude and self-care'),
('play-and-spt','Play-based communication and Synergetic Play Therapy'),
('creative-expression','Creative expression, art and music'),
('behavioral-support','Positive reinforcement, behavior tracking, behavioral intervention and behavioral activation'),
('cognitive-approaches','Cognitive challenging, reframing and restructuring'),
('everyday-skill-practice','Assertiveness, communication, boundaries, conflict resolution, interpersonal skills, role-play, behavioral rehearsal, social skills, structured problem solving, community support and time management'),
('trauma-informed-approach','Trauma-informed presence and specialist trauma treatment'),
('goals-and-plan','Emotional awareness, symptom management, psychoeducation, treatment-plan review and progress')]) + '</ul><p>Journaling, emotion identification, psychoeducation and self-care discussion may appear in an approved plan. Use authorized materials, consider privacy and accessibility, and do not ask for a journal to be shared beyond the agreed purpose. Physical wellness concerns and relationship-pattern treatment may require another qualified professional.</p>', 'learning-and-resources')
add('references-and-edition', 'References, further reading and this edition', f'''
<p>Adapted from the agency-owned Counseling Training Manual supplied on October 10, 2026. Repetition was consolidated, examples clarified and facilitator boundaries added. This edition preserves the original’s emphasis on relationships, children and teens, communication, play, creative expression, behavioral/cognitive approaches, common concerns, documentation, ethical practice and supervised learning. Examples can be discussed with supervisors across age groups; they are not age-specific treatment protocols.</p>
<ul><li>{link(MANUAL, 'HCPF January 2026 SBHS manual, H0004 p. 53')} and {link('https://hcpf.colorado.gov/bh-policies', 'current HCPF behavioral health policy index')}: dated billing and oversight references, not a coverage determination.</li>
<li>{link(RPO, 'HCPF Rendering Provider Oversight Policy')} and {link(FAQ, 'January 2026 FAQ')}: check applicability and current payer requirements.</li>
<li>{link(SAMHSA, 'SAMHSA basic communication guidance')} and {link(TIP, 'TIP 35, chapter 3')}: foundational helping concepts; the TIP’s context is substance-use treatment.</li>
<li>{link(WHO, 'WHO/UNICEF foundational helping skills manual, July 2025')}: trainer/supervisor reference for competency-based work with adults.</li>
<li>{link(BACB, 'BACB RBT Handbook, June 2026')}: a separate credential pathway, not counseling authorization.</li>
<li>{link(REPORT, 'CDHS/CO4Kids reporting guidance')}: current Colorado reporting information.</li></ul>
<p>Further reading from the original manual includes The Whole-Brain Child (Daniel J. Siegel and Tina Payne Bryson), Play Therapy: The Art of the Relationship (Garry L. Landreth), CBT Toolbox for Children and Adolescents, Helping Teens Who Cut (Michael Hollander), and work by Richard Nelson-Jones on counseling skills. These are reading references, not included curricula or authorized client protocols. Confirm the specific edition with supervision. No book pages, worksheets, proprietary therapy curricula or third-party assessments are reproduced here.</p>
<p>The original list of consumer apps is not an agency-approved tool list. Use the app’s approved resources and clinical record workflows; obtain approval for any additional tool, privacy arrangement or client resource. Reading recommendations do not grant reproduction or commercial redistribution rights.</p>
<p>Reference check: October 10, 2026. The HCPF sources identified here are dated January 2026 and the published RPO policy; their availability does not establish that they are the latest rules for every future service date. Administration verifies current state and payer rules before operational use. Company policy updates should be published as a new edition so source links and assigned training remain traceable.</p>''', 'learning-and-resources')

source = dict(documentKey='counseling-guide', title='Care Approaches & Practice Guide',
              organization='Company', sourceDate='2026-10-10', edition='Care approaches · Familiarization',
              learningPurpose='orientation', sections=sections)
(ROOT/'content/care-guides/shared.json').write_text(json.dumps(source, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)+'\n')

questions = [
('orientation', 'You passed this guide’s knowledge check. What does that establish?', ['Authorization to deliver every technique described.', 'Understanding of this orientation; clinical competence and authorization require separate review.', 'A professional therapy credential.'], 1, 'A knowledge check is orientation only. It does not certify techniques or authorize clinical practice.', 'care-orientation'),
('title', 'How should a bachelor’s-level staff member in the facilitator role introduce themselves?', ['As a therapist because H0004 includes that word.', 'As independently licensed under their supervisor’s license.', 'As a facilitator, accurately describing their role and licensed oversight.'], 2, 'The agency title is facilitator. A billing description does not confer a title or license.', 'facilitator-scope'),
('approval', 'A family asks a facilitator to treat a primary diagnosis using a new technique. What is required?', ['Express documented approval from the responsible qualified licensed clinician, plus lawful scope, demonstrated competence and an appropriate plan.', 'A parent’s request and a completed guide quiz.', 'Supervisor permission even if the activity is outside lawful scope.'], 0, 'Facilitators do not independently diagnose or begin diagnosis-targeted treatment. Approval cannot expand scope or substitute for competence.', 'facilitator-scope'),
('oversight', 'Which statement correctly describes the oversight requirement?', ['Two years in the field alone is enough.', 'A fully licensed, appropriately qualified provider with at least two years in the field, plus all applicable stricter board and payer requirements.', 'Any coworker who has read this guide can supervise.'], 1, 'The agency experience minimum is separate from licensing and Medicaid rendering-oversight qualifications.', 'licensed-oversight'),
('billing', 'A bachelor’s-level facilitator is listed in the H0004 reference. Does that make every scheduled visit billable?', ['Yes, if it lasted 30 scheduled minutes.', 'Yes, including tutoring during that time.', 'No. Verify actual service, eligibility, plan, medical necessity, authorizations, documentation and current payer rules.'], 2, 'H0004 is a service code, not blanket authorization or proof of coverage. Record actual service time.', 'h0004-service'),
('listening', 'A child says nobody wants them on their team. Which response fits the guide?', ['Tentatively reflect what you heard and invite their account, without assigning a diagnosis.', 'Promise that everyone likes them.', 'Tell them to replace the feeling with a positive thought.'], 0, 'Respectful reflection checks understanding and permits correction; it does not impose a feeling or diagnosis.', 'rapport-and-listening'),
('safety', 'An urgent safety concern emerges during an approved low-acuity activity. What should a facilitator do?', ['Wait until the next supervision meeting.', 'Follow emergency/reporting procedures and promptly contact qualified clinical support.', 'Independently decide the client is low risk and finish the exercise.'], 1, 'Urgent concerns require prompt escalation. Routine approval and the guide’s Q&A must not delay emergency action.', 'urgent-concerns')
]
required = ['care-orientation','care-role-map','facilitator-scope','licensed-oversight','h0004-service','facilitator-readiness','rapport-and-listening','questions-and-summaries','urgent-concerns','care-documentation','consent-and-privacy','competence-and-next-steps']
definition = dict(documentKey='counseling-guide', tenants=['*'], onboardingTenants=['itsco','nlu','isi'], onboardingAudience='clinical',
    title='Care Approaches & Practice Guide · Read & reflect', description='Care approaches, foundational communication, facilitator scope and Colorado H0004 oversight. Orientation only; not clinical authorization.',
    requiredSections=required, questions=[dict(id=i,prompt=p,options=o,correctAnswer=a,explanation=e,sourceSlug=s) for i,p,o,a,e,s in questions], passingScore=100,
    sectionHashes={s['slug']:hashlib.sha256(s['body_html'].strip().encode()).hexdigest() for s in sections if s['slug'] in required})
(ROOT/'content/document-training/counseling-guide.json').write_text(json.dumps(definition, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)+'\n')
subprocess.run(['node', '--input-type=module', '-e', '''
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';
import { cleanHandbookHtml } from './services/workplaceHandbookContent.js';
const path = './content/care-guides/shared.json', trainingPath = './content/document-training/counseling-guide.json';
const source = JSON.parse(readFileSync(path)), definition = JSON.parse(readFileSync(trainingPath));
source.sections.forEach(s => { s.body_html = cleanHandbookHtml(s.body_html.trim()); });
definition.sectionHashes = Object.fromEntries(source.sections.filter(s => definition.requiredSections.includes(s.slug)).map(s => [s.slug, createHash('sha256').update(s.body_html).digest('hex')]));
writeFileSync(path, JSON.stringify(source, null, 2) + '\\n');
writeFileSync(trainingPath, JSON.stringify(definition, null, 2) + '\\n');
'''], cwd=ROOT, check=True)
print(f'Built {len(sections)} care-guide sections and {len(questions)} knowledge-check questions.')
